/P 90 0 R
/E (Step 1: User Registration and Cryptographic Processing A new user registers by submitting their name, email address, and plaintext password. The authentication controller checks whether the email already exists in MongoDB. If unique, the user schema triggers a pre-save hook using bcrypt.js to generate an encrypted salt and hash of the password. The sanitized document is stored, and the server generates a signed JSON Web Token. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (Step 2: Credential Verification and Token Issuance During login, the user submits their registered email and password. The system searches MongoDB for a matching email record. If found, it compares the submitted password against the stored bcrypt hash. Once verified, the server issues a signed JWT containing the user ID, which the client retains for subsequent requests. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (Step 3: Route Protection and Request Interception When making requests to protected endpoints, the client includes the JWT in the Authorization Bearer header. Middleware intercepts the request, verifies the token's cryptographic integrity, extracts the user ID, and attaches it directly to the request object. Requests lacking a valid token are denied access immediately. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
